  message: "Cross-item review 2026-05-17: forms a cache-correctness cluster with INV-padum (7.9 GB unbounded growth) and WI-ribag (warm-cache sketch slow). INV-padum's description already calls out the coupling — this item's cache-miss rate × INV-padum's no-eviction = the observed bloat. WI-ribag is a separate axis (cache-hit but recomputation cost: centrality + per-file embeddings not in same cache layer). Fixing this item alone reduces INV-padum growth rate but does not bound it; fixing WI-ribag is orthogonal. Recommend tackling this first because it propagates the most pain into the others."  # 4ae4
- op: discuss  # fd76
  at: '2026-05-17T21:51:18Z'  # fd76
  by: agent  # fd76
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fd76
  clock: 3  # fd76
  nonce: fd76  # fd76
  message: "Cross-item review 2026-05-17: cross-reference to INV-tofur (AnalysisRun.repo_fingerprint is None on 100% of runs). That item turned out to be a producer gap for a spec-defined algorithm at docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:378-384, not a vestigial field. The spec's algorithm uses content-bytes-per-dirty-file (git_head + sha256(content_bytes) per dirty file), which is STABLE across mtime-only changes. By contrast, _get_repo_state_hash() at sketch_embeddings.py:1278-1376 (the function this item is about) uses git diff HEAD blob hash + path:size:mtime per untracked file — and the path:size:mtime branch is exactly what makes tracker .ops file churn pollute the cache shard, because every 'tracker show'/'tracker discuss' touches .ops file mtimes without changing content.\n\nCoupling opportunity: if INV-tofur is implemented by extracting a shared compute_repo_fingerprint(repo_root, dirty_files) function per the spec's content-hash algorithm, the cache layer in sketch_embeddings.py can migrate to call it. The migration fixes THIS item as a side effect because content hashes don't pick up mtime jitter the way path:size:mtime does.\n\nThis is a coordinated-fix opportunity, not a hard dependency: either item is fixable in isolation, but landing both together (via the shared function) is the structural fix that closes both with one architectural decision. Recorded on both items for symmetric findability."  # fd76
